<BaseLayout title="Video Compression & Codecs - François Pitié" currentSection="research">
  <div class="post">
    <header class="post-header mb-8">
      <div class="mb-2">
        <a href="/research" class="text-xs font-mono text-[var(--theme-link)] hover:text-[var(--theme-accent)]">&larr; back to research</a>
      </div>
      <h1 class="post-title">Video Compression &amp; Codecs</h1>
      <p class="desc text-xs sm:text-sm text-[var(--theme-muted)] mt-1.5 pb-3 border-b border-[var(--theme-border)]">
        Rate-distortion optimisation, open standards (AV1, AVM), one-pass encoder control, and codec-assisted 3D reconstruction.
      </p>
    </header>

    <article class="space-y-10">
      <!-- Overview -->
      <section class="space-y-3 text-sm sm:text-base text-[var(--theme-fg)] leading-relaxed">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-light tracking-tight text-[var(--theme-heading)] pb-1.5 border-b border-[var(--theme-border)]">
          Overview
        </h2>
        <p>
          Video accounts for the majority of internet traffic. As video moves to 4K/8K, HDR, and high frame rates, efficient compression becomes essential to keep bandwidth and storage manageable without sacrificing visual quality.
        </p>
        <p>
          My research in this area focuses on rate-distortion optimisation, perceptual quality metrics, fast encoder control, and finding ways to reuse information already calculated by video codecs for other tasks.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Open Standards & AOMedia -->
      <section class="space-y-3 text-sm sm:text-base text-[var(--theme-fg)] leading-relaxed">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-light tracking-tight text-[var(--theme-heading)] pb-1.5 border-b border-[var(--theme-border)]">
          Open Video Standards (AV1 &amp; AVM)
        </h2>
        <p>
          I represent Trinity College Dublin as an academic member of the <strong>Alliance for Open Media (AOMedia)</strong> Video Quality Working Group. Alongside partners like Google, Netflix, and Meta, our work contributes to the development and evaluation of open, royalty-free codecs, including <strong>AV1</strong> and its successor <strong>AVM (AOMedia Video Model)</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          In 2025, I gave an invited talk at the AOMedia Research Workshop Europe on applying AV1 to virtual production environments.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Rate-Distortion & HDR -->
      <section class="space-y-3 text-sm sm:text-base text-[var(--theme-fg)] leading-relaxed">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-light tracking-tight text-[var(--theme-heading)] pb-1.5 border-b border-[var(--theme-border)]">
          Rate-Distortion Optimisation &amp; HDR
        </h2>
        <p>
          Compressing High Dynamic Range (HDR) content requires adjusting encoder decisions because human sensitivity to contrast differs in HDR. Working with Google and YouTube, we developed methods to directly tune the Lagrangian multiplier (<span class="font-mono">&lambda;</span>) on a per-clip basis for AV1 encoding.
        </p>
        <p>
          This work evaluated perceptual metrics (such as VMAF and HDR-VQM) across thousands of HDR clips, receiving the Top-10 Papers Award at PCS 2021 and appearing in <em>IEEE Transactions on Broadcasting</em>. In related work published in the <em>SMPTE Motion Imaging Journal</em>, we investigated compression requirements for LED-wall virtual production studios.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- LiteVPNet & TrueFrame -->
      <section class="space-y-3 text-sm sm:text-base text-[var(--theme-fg)] leading-relaxed">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-light tracking-tight text-[var(--theme-heading)] pb-1.5 border-b border-[var(--theme-border)]">
          LiteVPNet &amp; TrueFrame: One-Pass Quality Prediction
        </h2>
        <p>
          Finding the best encoding parameters for a video usually requires running multiple test encodes, which wastes compute. We developed <strong>LiteVPNet</strong>, a compact neural network (242k parameters) that predicts suitable encoding parameters and perceptual quality (VMAF) in a single pass.
        </p>
        <p>
          Tested on 2,944 videos, LiteVPNet predicts VMAF within 1 point on average. This technology forms the core IP of <strong>TrueFrame</strong>, supported by the Research Ireland ARC Hub for ICT (€149.5k, 2026–2028).
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Codec-Assisted 3D Gaussian Splatting -->
      <section class="space-y-3 text-sm sm:text-base text-[var(--theme-fg)] leading-relaxed">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-light tracking-tight text-[var(--theme-heading)] pb-1.5 border-b border-[var(--theme-border)]">
          Codec-Assisted 3D Gaussian Splatting
        </h2>
        <p>
          3D Gaussian Splatting can reconstruct realistic 3D scenes from video, but it relies on an initial point cloud generated by expensive Structure-from-Motion (SfM) algorithms that match features across frames.
        </p>
        <p>
          In work accepted at <strong>IEEE ICIP 2026</strong> (Zouein et al.), we showed that you can bypass this expensive matching step by reusing the <strong>AV1 motion vectors</strong> already stored in the compressed video bitstream. This produces up to 8&times; more initial points, improves reconstruction quality by ~9 VMAF points, and reduces training time to reach baseline quality by 63%. The project was supported by Horizon Europe EMERALD and a YouTube/Google Faculty Award.
        </p>
      </section>

